The [[Parc Nacional de la Marjal]], covering 80.3 km² of coastal wetlands, is administered as a protected area and is not part of any municipality.

== Demographics ==

Els Aiguamolls had a population of 1,665,000 in 2026, making it the third most populous comarca in Estret Province after Sant Cristòfor and Cumará. The population density of 2,452/km² is the highest of any comarca in the province, reflecting the comarca's role as a densely developed bedroom community for the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area.

The ethnic composition of the comarca reflects its history as a suburban destination for Sant Cristòfor's middle and upper-middle classes. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] constitute the largest group, concentrated in the newer suburban developments of the interior and northern municipalities. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are concentrated in the older southern municipalities near the Sant Cristòfor border, particularly in La Riba and Els Canyars, descendants of workers who settled the area during the post-reunification industrial expansion. [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian Balboans]] are a significant minority in Vilanova and Sant Mateu, reflecting Zong-era settlement patterns. The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population is small, concentrated in the coastal communities near the wetlands.

The comarca's population has grown rapidly since the 1970s, driven by suburban expansion from Sant Cristòfor. The construction of the CristoCarril Line E corridor in the 1980s and 1990s accelerated residential development along the rail alignment, particularly in Vilanova, Els Canyars, and La Llacuna. The comarca's population surpassed one million in the early 2000s and has continued to grow at an average rate of approximately 2.5 per cent annually.

== Economy ==

The economy of Els Aiguamolls is dominated by its role as a bedroom community for [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The majority of the comarca's working population commutes to Sant Cristòfor for employment, using the CristoCarril Line E or the E1 expressway. The comarca's own employment base is concentrated in retail, education, healthcare, and municipal services, with limited industrial or agricultural activity.

Residential construction and property development are the primary domestic economic drivers. The comarca has experienced sustained residential building activity since the 1970s, with new housing estates and apartment complexes expanding the built-up area northward from the Sant Cristòfor border. The construction sector employs a significant portion of the comarca's resident workforce.

The coastal wetlands support small-scale fishing and aquaculture operations, particularly in Les Salines and La Riba. Salt production was historically significant in the Les Salines area — the municipality's name refers to the salt pans that operated along the coast from the colonial period until the mid-20th century. Tourism associated with the [[Parc Nacional de la Marjal]] and the coastal wetlands contributes modestly to the local economy, with visitor facilities concentrated in Les Salines.

The [[Sant Cristòfor Els Aiguamolls Airport]], a small domestic facility located in the municipality of Sant Cugat, provides limited passenger services. The airport primarily serves the northern districts of Sant Cristòfor and the Els Aiguamolls communities, with Aeroístmus flights connecting to [[Sant Bart]].

== Transport ==

=== Rail ===

{{main|Line E (CristoCarril)}}

[[CristoCarril]] [[Line E (CristoCarril)|Line E]] is the primary commuter rail service connecting Els Aiguamolls to Sant Cristòfor. The line operates on the [[Sant Bart Line]] corridor and serves three stations within the comarca:

