[[La Llacuna (CristoCarril)|La Llacuna]] station is the northernmost terminus of the CristoCarril system and provides a shuttle connection to [[L'Estany Airport]] in the neighbouring [[L'Estany (comarca)|L'Estany]] comarca. The express service between Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal and La Llacuna has a journey time of approximately 45 minutes, while the local service, stopping at all 17 stations, takes approximately 55 minutes.

=== Road ===

The [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|E1 expressway]], the principal north–south artery in Balboa, runs through the eastern portion of the comarca, connecting Sant Cristòfor to the northern comarcas of Estret and to Tramuntana Province beyond. The E1 provides the primary road connection between Els Aiguamolls and Sant Cristòfor, with interchanges serving Vilanova, Sant Joan, and Montclar. The [[Expressway 4 (Balboa)|E4 expressway]], which connects the E1 to the Aethiopian coast at Sant Llàtzer, passes through the southeastern portion of the comarca near Montclar, providing a cross-isthmus link.

A network of regional roads connects the inland municipalities to the coastal settlements and to the E1 corridor. The coastal road runs north–south along the Shendan shore, linking Les Salines, La Riba, and the wetland visitor facilities.

=== Air ===

{{main|Sant Cristòfor Els Aiguamolls Airport}}

The [[Sant Cristòfor Els Aiguamolls Airport]] (IATA: EAI, ICAO: EAIB) is located in the municipality of Sant Cugat. The airport handled approximately 80,000 passengers in 2025 and operates a single Aeroístmus route to [[Sant Bart]]. The airport primarily serves the northern districts of Sant Cristòfor and the Els Aiguamolls communities.

== History ==

The area of present-day Els Aiguamolls was originally inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]], who maintained fishing communities along the Shendan coast and in the wetlands. The coastal marshes provided rich fishing grounds, and the Birú practised seasonal fishing and gathering in the tidal flats and mangrove forests.

Volisanian colonists reached the area in the 16th century as part of the expansion of the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]]. The wetlands were initially used for fishing and salt production, with salt pans established at what is now Les Salines. The inland areas were cleared for cattle ranching and small-scale agriculture, though the poor drainage of the coastal plain limited large-scale cultivation.

During the [[Zong Balboa]] period (1899–1950), the comarca was part of the Zong-administered territory on the isthmus. The Zong administration invested little in the area, which remained rural and sparsely populated. The southern communities near the Riu Llobregat saw some growth as workers settled near the border with Sant Cristòfor, but the interior and northern portions of the comarca remained agricultural land and wetland.

The [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor]] (1950–1960) brought the area under Adonian Community administration. The mandate period saw the first conservation measures for the coastal wetlands, with the [[Parc Nacional de la Marjal]] formally designated in 1956, protecting the marshes from further development and establishing the basis for the modern nature reserve.

Following the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) and the reunification of Estret Province, the comarca experienced rapid suburban development. The extension of the E1 expressway through the comarca in the late 1970s and early 1980s opened the area to residential development, and the construction of the CristoCarril Line E corridor in the 1980s and 1990s accelerated population growth along the rail alignment. The comarca's population grew from approximately 200,000 in 1970 to over one million by the early 2000s, transforming the formerly rural area into a densely populated suburban belt.

The rapid suburbanisation has created challenges for the comarca's wetland ecosystems. Urban runoff and drainage modifications have affected the hydrology of the coastal marshes, and the Balboan National Parks Service has implemented conservation measures to protect the remaining wetland areas. The Parc Nacional de la Marjal was designated a {{w|Ramsar site|wetland of international importance}} in 1998, recognising its ecological significance.

